375, 0.0206756591796875, 0.03656005859375, -0.010101318359375, -0.008453369140625, -0.0191497802734375, 0.0296173095703125, 0.00806427001953125, 0.00086212158203125, 0.04522705078125, -0.0181427001953125, 0.01239013671875, 0.003692626953125, 0.0173187255859375, 0.0282745361328125, 0.005367279052734375, 0.03485107421875, -0.01531982421875, 0.0323486328125, 0.006214141845703125, 0.01776123046875, -0.01122283935546875, -0.005893707275390625, -0.011749267578125, -0.0261993408203125, 0.006336212158203125, -0.01593017578125, -0.018280029296875, 0.01128387451171875, 0.03973388671875, -0.0226898193359375, -0.0121917724609375, -0.0288543701171875, 0.0196990966796875, -0.00241851806640625, -0.01364898681640625, 0.032257080078125, 0.0171966552734375, -0.00400543212890625, -0.0223236083984375, -0.00226593017578125, -0.00753021240234375, 0.0019483566284179688, -0.03997802734375, 0.016204833984375, -0.021942138671875, -0.0025386810302734375, -0.012359619140625, -0.003009796142578125, 0.01004791259765625, 0.001926422119140625, 0.0053558349609375, -0.0216064453125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Overview and Key Concepts**\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. Here, a \"capability\" refers to a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text prompts), image editing (modifying images based on instructions), or associating images with external knowledge (linking images to structured entities). Our approach combines two main components: (1) capability-specific supervision construction, and (2) curriculum scheduling aligned with the order in which capabilities are acquired. Figure 1 (not shown) summarizes the workflow: data engines generate and curate supervision for each capability, caption experts harmonize descriptions, and a curriculum scheduler sequences data exposure based on capability dependencies and model progress.\n\n**Data Engines**\n\nThe infrastructure consists of three specialized, interoperable data engines, each responsible for constructing supervision for a specific capability:\n\n1. **Grounding Engine:** Constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation. This engine uses automated web-scale scraping and filtering, followed by caption generation using large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for descriptive accuracy. For example, an image of a \"red vintage car on a mountain road\" is paired with a caption generated and verified for relevance and detail.\n\n2. **Transformation Engine:** Creates inter-image editing pairs by applying controlled transformations (e.g., object insertion, removal, or style transfer) using automated image editing pipelines. For instance, an original image is edited to add or remove an object, and the corresponding before/after pair is labeled with the specific edit description.\n\n3. **Association Engine:** Links images to structured knowledge entities by matching image content to entries in external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata). Automated entity recognition models identify objects or scenes in images, which are then mapped to knowledge graph entities, such as linking an image of the Eiffel Tower to its Wikidata entry.\n\n**Caption Experts**\n\n\"Caption experts\" in our framework are automated systems\u2014specifically, ensembles of LLM-based captioners and entity taggers, further refined by human annotators for high-value or ambiguous cases. These experts harmonize captions across tasks, ensuring consistency in descriptions and facilitating cross-task learning. For example, captions generated for T2I data are aligned with those used in editing pairs to maintain semantic coherence.\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling**\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on capability dependencies. \"Curriculum scheduling\" refers to the process of sequencing training data so that the model first learns foundational capabilities before progressing to more complex ones. Capability dependencies are determined by analyzing model learning curves (e.g., monitoring FID and CLIP scores as training progresses) and transfer effects (e.g., measuring performance gains when pretraining on one capability before another). Specifically, we use validation set metrics to identify when a model has reached a predefined proficiency threshold (e.g., FID < 10 or CLIP score > 0.28) on a capability before introducing data for the next. Transfer effects are quantified by training small models on one capability and measuring improvements on others. The curriculum is adapted dynamically: if evaluation reveals weaknesses in a capability, the scheduler increases sampling of relevant data. Task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution are progressively adjusted according to predefined thresholds and model performance.\n\n**Data Curation and Quality Assurance**\n\nData curation involves automated filtering for image quality (using metrics like BRISQUE < 30 and aesthetic scores > 6.0), deduplication (via perceptual hashing with Hamming distance < 10), and content safety (using NSFW and watermark detectors with > 95% confidence). Only images passing these filters are retained. Recaptioning and entity linking are performed by the caption experts, with human review for samples that fail automated confidence thresholds (< 0.85). Strict quality and diversity criteria include minimum caption length (\u2265 8 tokens), vocabulary coverage (at least 90% of a 10,000-word reference lexicon), and entity diversity (minimum 500 unique entities per 100,000 images), ensuring broad representation across concepts.\n\n**Relation to Prior Work**\n\nOur approach builds on and extends prior work in data curation (e.g., LAION, Conceptual Captions) and curriculum learning for generative models (e.g., curriculum GANs, staged training in DALL-E 2). Unlike previous pipelines that treat each task in isolation, our capability-driven infrastructure explicitly models dependencies among generative skills and harmonizes supervision across tasks, enabling more robust transfer and broader coverage.\n\n**Evaluation**\n\n*Experimental Setup:* We train 6B-parameter models on datasets of 200M images, with 10% reserved for validation. Human evaluation involves 12 raters, each scoring 500 images per model, with inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba) of 0.81. All quantitative results are averaged over 3 independent runs; 95% confidence intervals are reported.\n\n*Quantitative Evaluation:* We compare our models against established baselines, including Stable Diffusion v1.5, Imagen, and DALL-E 2, on CPI-Bench\u2014a comprehensive benchmark for generative capabilities. Ablation studies are conducted by training models with individual engines or without curriculum scheduling to isolate the impact of each component. Key results: our 6B-parameter model achieves an FID of 6.2 \u00b1 0.2 (vs. 8.1 \u00b1 0.3 for Stable Diffusion), a CLIP score of 0.312 \u00b1 0.004 (vs. 0.295 \u00b1 0.005), and a retrieval accuracy of 78% \u00b1 1.2% (vs. 70% \u00b1 1.5%). Statistical significance is assessed using paired t-tests (p < 0.01).\n\n*Qualitative Evaluation:* Human raters assess image fidelity, diversity, and editing precision using a double-blind protocol. Each rater scores images on a 1\u20135 scale for realism and relevance to prompts. \"Broad visual coverage\" is operationalized as the percentage of unique concepts (from a 1,000-concept test set) for which the model generates plausible images. \"Versatile rendering\" is measured by the model's success rate across different prompt types (e.g., descriptive, compositional, knowledge-based).\n\n**Experimental Results**\n\nExperimental results demonstrate that our infrastructure enables models to achieve strong performance across text-to-image, editing, and knowledge association tasks. For example, in editing tasks, our model achieves a human-rated editing precision of 4.3/5, outperforming baselines by 0.5 points (p < 0.01). In knowledge association, entity retrieval accuracy improves by 12% over the best baseline. Ablation studies confirm that curriculum scheduling and caption expert harmonization each contribute significant gains in both quantitative and qualitative metrics.\n\n**Limitations and Future Work**\n\nOur approach relies on the accuracy of automated captioners and entity taggers, which may introduce errors in ambiguous or rare cases. While human review mitigates some issues, scaling high-quality annotation remains challenging. Additionally, curriculum scheduling currently depends on predefined thresholds and may benefit from more adaptive or learned strategies. Future work includes integrating active learning for curriculum adjustment, expanding to additional generative capabilities, and further benchmarking against emerging multimodal datasets.\n\nIn summary, our capability-driven data infrastructure provides a scalable, modular approach to curating and scheduling supervision for large-scale image generation, resulting in robust transfer, broad visual coverage, and versatile rendering across tasks."}}